# The other direction of the same 14400 call, with modec answering.
#
# The far end of this one is the Banksia itself, calling; it is the only
# recording in the corpus that speaks first, and therefore the only one
# modec can answer.  Every other live fixture is role: originate because
# a recording cannot answer a modem that speaks first.
#
# Worth having separately from its twin.  Figure 4 is not symmetric --
# the calling receiver gets two conditioning signals and the answering
# receiver one -- and the answering ladder was where the timing work
# went wrong twice: once by narrowing the loop on the caller's AA, which
# is a steady tone and says nothing about symbol timing, and once by
# missing the caller's E by a single bit, E being sent once where a rate
# signal repeats.  Neither showed up calling out.
#
# The text here is the other half of the same V.42 exchange: the
# Banksia's originator detection pattern, 0x11 0x91 over and over, then
# its LAPM frames.  Byte for byte, from a recording nothing at this end
# produced -- see the other file for what the empty reference this used
# to carry was really pinning.  Retrains: 0, for the reasons there.
source:    recordings/third-party/banksia-mymodem144-call.wav
seconds:   32.0
role:      answer
modes:     v32bis
v8:        no
connect:   V32bis 14400
retrains:  0
tolerance: 0
